\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Length}(P_\gamma)=
 \sum_{j=0}^{N-1}(2\pi/N)\,
 \operatorname{speed}(P_\gamma|_{[2\pi j/N,2\pi(j+1)/N]}).
 \label{eq:polygon-length}
\end{equation}
For a pointwise null family, the raw polygons have genuine filling
disks and filling-area error less than $\zeta$. Fixed-profile
flattening gives a family smooth in the angular coordinate, with
continuous first and second angular jets, freely homotopic to the
original family in the $C^1$ loop space.
\source{MT2007}\dcref{Definition 19.18}
\dcref{Claims 19.19--19.20}
\dcref{Corollary 19.21, pp. 450--451}
\dcref{Lemma 19.17}
\dcref{Claim 19.22, pp. 449--453}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Compactness gives a common geodesically convex neighborhood of the
diagonal and uniform continuity of the family's first angular
derivatives. Fine meshes therefore give unique short geodesic sides
with uniformly small chord deficits. The interpolation's transverse
derivative tends uniformly to zero, while its angular derivative
stays bounded, so its Jacobian area tends uniformly to zero.

Flatten each side using the same smooth increasing transition, flat
at its endpoints. This preserves its image and length and matches all
angular derivatives at the vertices. A short geodesic homotopy from
the original loops to the flattened loops is continuous in $C^1$.
The flattening is a circle homeomorphism; absorb it into each disk's
boundary label rather than precomposing its interior by a nonsmooth
inverse. Gluing the small interpolation annuli to actual disks in
both directions gives the filling-area error.
\end{proof}

